# crypt — the undercroft: a rib-vaulted hall crypt hollowed out of the solid podium.
#
# Forty short granite columns on a 2.70 bay carrying a RIBBED vault 2.5625 m over a travertine
# floor, inside the podium mass and 0.15 m of made ground under it, from y = -0.875 to y = 1.725.
# Two stairs down from the passage under the interior grand stair; three light-wells reaching south
# under the portico, each a segmental barrel 1.80 m wide with a moulded archivolt on its mouth,
# grated with iron bars; eight niches with tapers; four tomb chests with blind arcading and an
# incised inscription; a water channel the length of the room from a spouted basin to a sump under
# the middle grating. It is lit by 3.6 square metres of grating and by eight tapers, and by nothing
# else at all.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — do not "tidy" any of it away.
#
# 1. THE DEEPEST HOLLOW IN THE BUILDING AND THE SMALLEST OPENINGS IN IT, BY AN ORDER OF MAGNITUDE.
#
#    266.8 m2 of floor, 2.56 m of head height, and the entire daylight supply is three openings of
#    1.20 x 1.00 m — 3.60 m2 — each of them HALF SHUT by its own bars, each of them at the far end
#    of a 3.30 m corridor, and each of them opening not into the sky but into the shade under the
#    portico, which portico.clip's own header calls the deepest exterior shadow in the building.
#    So the light that reaches this floor has bounced off the portico's paving, come down a shaft,
#    passed a grating, and travelled the length of an unlit corridor before it arrives.
#
#    The rotunda is the building's famous deep-hollow case: an oculus 1.80 m across and sixteen drum
#    windows over a room 9.60 m wide. Per square metre of floor this room has about one fiftieth of
#    its opening, and every bit of that opening is in shadow to begin with. The rotunda asks whether
#    indirect light is the right COLOUR. This room asks whether it ARRIVES.
#
#    THEREFORE: IF THIS ROOM COMES BACK BLACK EXCEPT FOR THE FLAMES, THAT IS THE ANSWER AND NOT A
#    BUG IN THIS FILE. Do not widen the wells, do not add a window and do not add a lamp to make it
#    easier to look at. The room is deliberately at the far end of the range; a renderer that gets
#    nothing down here is telling you something a brighter room cannot.
#
# 2. A HARD SHADOW PATTERN FROM ONE-VOXEL BARS ONTO A ROUGH FLOOR.
#
#    Each grating is seven bars 0.030 m thick at 0.150 m centres. At the contract's metre 32 a
#    voxel is 0.03125 m, so A BAR IS ONE VOXEL — under one — and the gap between two is under four.
#    What that draws on the travertine below is a stencil of seven hard-edged stripes in a room
#    that is otherwise black, and it is the highest-contrast small feature in the building.
#
#    Three separate failures show up here and NOWHERE ELSE IN THIS SCENE, because nothing else in
#    the building is a one-voxel occluder with a metre of air behind it:
#      - a sampler that loses a bar closes the stripe: six stripes instead of seven, or a smear.
#      - a shadow ray with too much bias softens the stripe's edge, and a stripe cast 2.57 m from
#        a 0.03 m bar has an edge sharp enough that any softening is visible at once.
#      - a denoiser trained on smooth gradients eats the whole pattern and returns a grey pool.
#    DO NOT THICKEN THE BARS. 0.030 is the number; it is one voxel and it is meant to be.
#
# 3. FORTY NEARLY IDENTICAL COLUMNS, WHICH MUST NOT LOOK IDENTICAL — repetition in DEPTH.
#
#    Thirty-two free-standing and eight engaged, all one shape, on a 2.70 grid 23.40 m long. From
#    the foot of either stair you look down eight bays of it: the near shafts are 0.45 m of solid
#    voxels and the far ones are a few pixels wide, in one frame, with nothing between them but
#    more of the same. That is the variation case (the no-two-voxels-alike rule has forty chances
#    to fail here and one failure makes the whole colonnade read as stamped metal) and the
#    continuous-detail case (a level-of-detail step inside a colonnade is the most visible place
#    such a step can happen, because the eye walks past the transition rather than looking at it).
#    Every column is placed by a rigid motion — repeat, translate, mirror — so the field stays
#    exact and any difference between two of them in a render came from the renderer.
#
# 4. GRAZING LIGHT ON WET STONE, AND NOTHING AT MIDDLE DISTANCE.
#
#    A taper stands 0.075 m off a tuff wall at rough=250 in each of eight niches: that is a point
#    source raking a rough surface at a few degrees, which is the case that either draws the
#    surface's own grain or flattens it. Twelve metres away the same flame is a spark. There is
#    water on the floor between them — a channel 0.225 m wide running 13.58 m from a basin to a
#    sump, at ior 1.33 and rough 24, with a 0.135 m thread falling 1.25 m into the basin from a
#    bronze spout — so the one polished surface in the room is horizontal, dark, and lit only by
#    things that are either very close or very far. Everything in here is one or the other.
#
# 5. AND NOW A COURSED WALL AND AN INCISED LETTER, WHICH ARE THE SUB-VOXEL CASE STATED PLAINLY.
#
#    The lining is laid in courses of M with joints 0.030 wide and 0.030 deep — ONE VOXEL at the
#    contract's metre, cut into a face that a taper rakes at a few degrees. The tomb chests carry
#    blind arcading sunk 0.030 and an inscription whose strokes are 0.036 across. Both are matter
#    and not paint, because a 30 mm joint in stone is matter; and both disappear entirely at metre
#    8, which is the honest answer and the reason the contract's metre is 32.
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE HEAD HEIGHT, WHICH IS WHY THE FLOOR IS WHERE IT IS.
#
#   bed          -0.875    the floor slab's top: a voxel boundary at metre 8, 16 AND 32
#   FLOOR        -0.845    the flags laid on it, 0.030 thick — worn through where feet fall
#   springing     1.6875   1.80 less M/4: the plane the vault's ribs and webs spring from
#   rib soffit    1.587    the ribs hang M/4 below the springing
#   web crown     1.725    the webs are cut UP, 1.6875 + M/12
#
#   clear         2.5325 to the springing plane, 2.57 to a web crown, 2.4325 under a rib
#
#   MEASURED at metre 8: 2.500 under a rib and in every aisle, 2.625 under a web crown. At metre 16
#   and 32 the worst reading anywhere a person can stand is 2.4375.
#
# THE ROOM WAS 2.15 m AND IS NOW 2.5325, AND THE 0.3825 CAME OFF THE BOTTOM BECAUSE THERE IS
# NOTHING TO GIVE AT THE TOP. rotunda.clip's floor is a cylinder of radius 5.10 whose underside is
# at 1.665, fifteen millimetres above my springing plane; vestibule.clip's floor block hangs to
# 1.35; halls.clip and stair.clip are founded at 1.75. A ceiling raised past 1.725 opens holes in
# the floor of the storey above. So the floor went down instead, into made ground: site.clip's own
# slab is solid from -0.90 to 0.00 and the podium's plinth only reaches -0.45, so everything below
# -0.45 here is a cellar cut into earth, which is what an undercroft is.
#
# WHY -0.875 AND NOT A MODULE NUMBER. Two sampler rules, neither of them the room's:
#
#   A VOXEL IS DECIDED AT ITS CENTRE. A face that does not land on a voxel boundary gives the whole
#   of the straddled voxel to whichever side its centre falls, so a room drawn to exactly 2.40
#   measures anything from 2.31 to 2.46 depending on the metre. -0.875 is 7 voxels at metre 8, 14
#   at metre 16 and 28 at metre 32: A BOUNDARY AT ALL THREE, which no multiple of M under -0.45 is.
#
#   THE BOX HAS A FLOOR AND IT MOVES. The contract's `bounds` start at y = -0.90, and the sampler
#   rounds that outward to the enclosing voxel — -1.000 at metre 8, -0.9375 at 16, -0.90625 at 32.
#   A room whose floor is at -0.90 has NO STONE UNDER IT at metre 32 and reports its clear run OPEN
#   at the near end. So the floor is -0.875 and the block runs to -1.05, which is under the box at
#   every metre and is therefore one solid row of stone under the floor at every metre.
#
#   floor    springing    metre 8   metre 16   metre 32
#   -0.4625   1.6875      2.125     2.125      2.156     what was built before
#   -0.75     1.6875      2.375     2.4375     2.4375    fails metre 8 by 25 mm
#   -0.845    1.6875      2.500     2.5625     2.53125   WHAT IS BUILT, between the ribs
#   -0.845    1.5870      2.500     2.4375     2.46875   WHAT IS BUILT, under a rib
#
# THE RIBS HANG M/4 AND NOT MORE, AND THAT IS THE HEAD HEIGHT AND NOT TASTE. A rib soffit at 1.587
# leaves 2.4375 under it at metre 16 and 2.500 at metre 8; one at 1.5625 measures 2.375 at metre 8
# and fails. The webs go the other way — cut UP from the springing plane to 1.725, which is where
# rotunda.clip's paving still has 0.075 m of marble over it — so they ADD height rather than take
# it. Everything in this ceiling is between those two numbers.
#
# TWO SAMPLER RULES THAT THIS FILE HAD WRITTEN DOWN WRONG, AND BOTH WERE CAUGHT BY A COMPONENT
# COUNT RATHER THAN BY READING:
#
#   `round=` DOES NOT GROW A BOX. It is `sd_box(p, half - r) - r`, which keeps the extent and turns
#   the arrises inside it — so a box written to a face IS at that face. This file used to say the
#   opposite and wrote every stair number 0.012 inside its own face to compensate, which made the
#   treads 12 mm shallow. They are written at their true faces now.
#
#   TWO SOLIDS THAT MEET ON A PLANE ARE TWO SOLIDS. A sample is taken at the voxel's centre and a
#   point exactly on a face is not inside it, so a plinth whose top and a chest whose bottom are
#   both 0.6875 leave the row between them to neither: `--part crypt_sarcophagi` came back as EIGHT
#   components, four chests and four plinths. Every member here now overlaps the one under it IN
#   RANGE, which is what enfilade.clip's vessels have always said and this file had not.
#
# AND IT IS A REAL VAULT AND NOT A COFFER, WHICH IS WORTH SAYING BECAUSE IT LOOKS LIKE ONE IN PLAN.
# Each web is the union of two barrels of radius 16.894 struck on the bay's own axes and crowning
# 0.0375 over their springing — so the web is DOMED in both directions and the two barrels meet on
# the bay's diagonals, which is what a groin is. A single flat step would have been three lines
# shorter and would have had no groins in it at all. The rise is small because the podium is only
# 2.30 m thick; the geometry is a groined web whatever the rise, and a shallow one is what every
# real undercroft under a heavy building actually has.
#
# Three consequences, and each one is why something below is shaped the way it is:
#
#   THE COLUMNS ARE CLOSE AND THE VAULT IS FLAT. A semicircular groin vault over a 2.70 bay needs
#   1.35 m of rise and would have to spring 3.75 m over the floor to keep 2.40 under its haunches;
#   the podium has 2.30 m of stone in it altogether. So the webs rise 0.0375 and the ribs carry the
#   reading, which is what a hall crypt under a piano nobile is and why this room is hypostyle and
#   the halls upstairs are not.
#
#   THE WEBS ARE CUT UPWARDS AND ONLY 0.0375. rotunda.clip's floor is a cylinder 0.135 thick whose
#   underside is at 1.665. A web 0.30 deep would open twenty-one holes in the floor of the rotunda.
#   0.0375 leaves that floor 0.075 m of marble over each of the nine webs it covers — never seen,
#   and never walked through either. DO NOT DEEPEN THEM without re-reading rotunda.clip line 134.
#
#   THE STAIR'S ODD RISER IS AT THE TOP AND NOT AT THE BOTTOM. 1.80 down to -0.845 is 2.645 m,
#   which is 14.69 risers of the building's 0.18, so one riser has to be 0.125 and there is no
#   arrangement in which it does not. Putting the 0.125 at the FOOT needs a landing 0.125 above the
#   floor, which is a lip in a walking route for nothing. So: fourteen risers of 0.18 from the floor
#   at -0.845 up to 1.675, and a last step of 0.125 onto the passage floor at 1.80, taken at the
#   threshold where you are already holding the door jamb. Every riser in the flight is under the
#   0.55 a person can step, and the odd one is the smallest of the fifteen.
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45, bay = 3 D = 2.70. Every number is one of these or a simple
# fraction, and the ones that are not are the building's own constants named where they occur —
# the riser 0.18, the run 0.32, the 0.05 overlap and the 0.02 paint test — or the two voxel
# boundaries above.
#
#   +-11.70  = 26 M       room east/west face   -1.05   block underside, 0.15 under the site slab
#   +-11.85  = 26 M + M/3 block back            -0.845  FLOOR, the flags     bed -0.875 + 0.030
#   +- 5.70  = 13 M - M/3 room north/south face -0.5625 column plinth top    -0.45 - M/4
#   +- 5.85  = 13 M       block back            -0.45   splay head, shaft foot, podium underside
#
#   THE WALL IS M/3 THICK AND THE MODULE IS TAKEN ON A DIFFERENT FACE IN EACH DIRECTION, which is
#   worth saying out loud because it looks like a slip. In x the ROOM is on the module — 11.70 is
#   26 M exactly — and the block's back falls where the lining puts it. In z the BLOCK is on the
#   module — 5.85 is 13 M — and the room's face falls M/3 inside it. It is that way round because
#   the zone stops at z = +-6.00 and the niches are 0.30 deep: a room face on 5.85 would put their
#   backs at 6.15 and outside it. The same argument podium.clip makes about its die: the module
#   sizes the mass, and a member's own thickness decides which face of it the number lands on.
#      2.70  =  6 M   the bay                    1.350 cushion capital foot
#      0.45  =  M     column diameter, D/2       1.545 abacus foot
#      0.225 =  M/2   column radius              1.575 RIB SOFFIT           1.6875 - M/4
#      0.675 = 1.5 M  plinth, square             1.6875 SPRINGING PLANE     1.80 - M/4
#      1.80  =  4 D   the shaft — the Ionic is 9 1.725  WEB CROWN           1.6875 + M/12
#      2.25  =  5 M   the web, square            1.80   podium top, floor above
#      3.60  =  8 M   the light-well barrel      0.030  grating bar = ONE VOXEL at metre 32
#
# The column axes are at odd multiples of 1.35 in BOTH directions — x = +-1.35, +-4.05, +-6.75,
# +-9.45 and z = +-1.35, +-4.05 — so the grid is one square lattice of the bay with the origin at
# the centre of a bay. That does two things at once: the x axes are the contract's own six portico
# numbers carried on by two more, so every shaft down here stands under a shaft or a pilaster up
# there; and it leaves a clear aisle along x = 0 and along z = 0, which is what you look down when
# you arrive, and the origin overhead is where the player spawns. THE RIBS ARE ON THOSE SAME AXES,
# so every rib springs off a capital and none of them crosses a bay.
#
# FORTY COLUMNS, AND EIGHT OF THEM ARE ENGAGED. 8 x 4 = 32 free-standing, and the four rows are
# each stopped against the east and west walls by an engaged half-column on the same axes, which
# is 8 more. A fifth free row would have to stand at z = +-5.40, 0.225 m off the wall, which is a
# slot and not an aisle; a ninth column in each row would have to stand at x = +-12.15 and leave
# the zone. The responds are how the count is honoured without either, and they are also what the
# four transverse ribs die into.
#
# WHERE IT MEETS ITS NEIGHBOURS
#
#   site.clip       the made ground. site_ground is solid -0.90 .. 0.00 over the whole site, so
#                   everything of mine below the podium's -0.45 is a cellar cut into that slab and
#                   lined with my own stone. My block runs 0.15 below it, under the sampled box.
#   podium.clip     everything above -0.45. This room is a hole in podium_stone; my lining and my
#                   ceiling slab sit inside it and are painted over it.
#   the floors above  vestibule 1.80 (block down to 1.35), rotunda 1.80 (down to 1.665), halls and
#                   stair founded at 1.75. My ceiling slab runs 1.6875..1.80 and meets all of them.
#   stair.clip      the two stair wells open in the passage under the interior grand stair, at
#                   z 4.575..5.70 — clear of its spandrel, which ends at z = 4.485, by 0.09 — and
#                   under its upper flight, whose soffit is at 3.915 and which touches no floor.
#   portico.clip    the three gratings are in the portico paving at z -9.00..-8.00 on x = 0 and
#                   +-4.05. Its columns stand at z = -11.15 and its antae at x = +-6.75, so
#                   nothing of its is over a well.
#
# THE ONE PLACE THIS PART LEAVES ITS ZONE, SAID PLAINLY. The room is inside x +-12, z +-6 as
# briefed, and now runs from -0.875 rather than -0.45 in y. The three light-wells are not: the
# brief puts them under the portico at z -9.00..-8.00, and a well 2.30 m north of the room it
# lights is a sealed pit. So each is a corridor 1.80 m wide running south from the room's south
# wall at z = -5.70 to z = -9.00, passing under the main south wall in a slot with 0.10 m of podium
# over its crown and M/3 of lining either side of it. It removes podium and made-ground mass and
# touches no other fragment's geometry: nothing else in the building is inside the podium or under
# it at all. IT WAS 1.20 m WIDE AND FLAT-CEILINGED and it is 1.80 and segmentally vaulted, which is
# the difference between a service duct a person turns sideways in and a passage.
#
# MEASURED, with `clips/facility/requests/crypt-probe.clip` at metre 8, which is the metre the
# walkability gate is asked at. `--gap y@x,z` reports the longest unbroken run of air on a column
# and whether stone closed it at both ends; the point of each of these is that it is taken where
# head height FAILS in a vaulted room — at the springing and hard against a wall — and not down the
# middle where it is easy.
#
#                                            before   after
#   bay centre, under a web crown             2.000    2.625
#   under a rib, on a column axis             2.000    2.500
#   0.30 m from the east wall                 2.000    2.500
#   0.05 m from the east wall                 2.000    2.500
#   0.05 m from the north wall                2.000    2.500
#   light-well corridor, centre line          1.875    2.500
#   light-well corridor, 0.60 off centre      1.875    2.500
#   light-well corridor, AT THE SPRINGING     1.875    2.500
#   over the first tread of the stair            --    2.250
#
#   clear width between two columns on a row     --    2.250 (`--gap x@0.5,1.35`)
#   clear width of a light-well corridor       1.125    1.875 (`--gap x@-3.00,-7.30`, on the
#                                                              assembled building, whose podium is
#                                                              what closes the run at both ends)
#
# And the three lanes of `crypt_walk_*` above — 1.20 m wide, from 0.30 over the flags to 2.40 over
# them, down both long aisles, both cross aisles and all three corridors — measure EMPTY at metre 32.
#
# Three of the before readings came back flagged `-- UNDER THE 2.10 m HEAD HEIGHT` by clipcheck
# itself. None of the after readings is under 2.40. `--part crypt_sarcophagi` reported EIGHT
# components before the plinth and the chest were made to overlap in range and ONE after.
#
# THE VOID TRAP, WHICH THIS FILE IS FULL OF THINGS THAT WOULD FALL INTO IT. void_crypt is the air
# of this room LESS MY OWN STONE, and the ribs, the gratings, the tomb chests, the tapers, the
# water, the spout, the archivolts and the whole stair stand IN that air. Every one of them is in
# part_crypt, so every one of them is subtracted back out of the void. Read the comment at the
# bottom of clips/facility.clip: the last time this was got wrong the building lost every sconce,
# bench and statue it had.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE AIR — the room, the light-well barrels, the shafts, the stair wells, the niches, the webs
# ================================================================================================
#
# Named first because in this part the air is the room and everything else is a lining round it.

let crypt_room = box -11.70 -0.845 -5.70   11.70 1.6875 5.70

# --- the light-well corridors and their shafts ---------------------------------------------------
#
# One corridor drawn at x = 0, copied to x = 4.05 and folded, so all three are the same shape.
# It runs 0.10 PAST the room's south face at -5.70, to -5.60, so the room and the corridor are
# certainly one body of air rather than two boxes touching on a plane.
#
# 1.80 m WIDE AND SEGMENTALLY VAULTED, AND THE RADIUS IS THE HEAD HEIGHT ARITHMETIC. A semicircular
# barrel over a 1.80 m corridor springs 0.90 below its crown, which puts the soffit 0.60 m from the
# centre line at 1.485 and leaves 2.36 m of head over the middle metre of it — under the 2.40 the
# circulation rule wants. A segmental barrel of radius 3.60 (8 M) crowning on the springing plane
# drops only 0.0504 at 0.60 from the centre, so the middle 1.20 m of the corridor has 2.50 m over
# it at metre 8 and the springing at the wall is 1.5732, which is 2.448 in the clear. Measured, not
# assumed: `--gap y@0,-7.30` and `--gap y@0.60,-7.30`.
#
# The shaft over its far end goes to 1.85 — 0.05 above the podium top — so the opening is cut clean
# through the paving and past the rounding on its arris, and the grating sits in a 0.15 m reveal.
# It starts at 1.60, which is under the barrel's soffit at every x it covers, so shaft and corridor
# are one body of air.

let crypt_slot_air_0 = intersection {
    cylinder 0 -1.9125 -7.30  r=3.60 h=3.80 axis=z
    box -0.90 -0.845 -9.00    0.90 1.6875 -5.60
}
let crypt_slot_shaft_0 = box -0.60 1.60 -9.00    0.60 1.85 -8.00
let crypt_slot_hollow_0 = union { crypt_slot_air_0 crypt_slot_shaft_0 }

let crypt_slot_hollows = mirror {
    union { crypt_slot_hollow_0  translate { crypt_slot_hollow_0 } 4.05 0 0 }
} axis=x

# --- the stair wells -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A hole in the ceiling only: 4.05 long, 1.125 wide (2.5 M), from 1.5625 to 1.85, so it cuts the
# ribs, my own slab and the podium over it and opens in the passage floor at 1.80. The stair itself
# is inside the room and needs no air of its own.
#
# IT STARTS AT 1.5625 AND NOT AT 1.60, AND THAT IS THE RIBS. A rib hangs to 1.575 and the rib on
# x = +-9.45 runs straight over both stairs; a well that started above the rib's soffit would cut
# the rib in half and leave a 0.0125 ribbon of it adrift. 1.5625 is under the whole rib section, so
# the well takes each rib away cleanly at z = 4.575 and the rib dies on the edge of the opening.
#
# IT REACHES 0.43 WEST OF THE FIRST NOSING ON PURPOSE. The third tread's top is at -0.335, so a
# head on it is at 1.465 and needs the ceiling gone; the two treads below it have 2.375 and 2.125
# under the springing plane and do not. 7.65 is where those two meet, and it is 17 M.
let crypt_well_e = box 7.65 1.5625 4.575   11.70 1.85 5.70
let crypt_wells  = mirror { crypt_well_e } axis=x

# --- the niches ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 0.60 wide (4 M/3), 0.30 deep (2 M/3), sill at 0.45 (M), semicircular head springing at 1.05 and
# crowning at 1.35. Eight of them, on x = +-2.70 and +-5.40 in both long walls: FEW AND FAR APART,
# as the brief asks, because eight flames in a room this size is already a lot of light and
# because a niche every bay would turn the wall into an arcade and the room into a corridor.
# They are 0.30 deep and the lining is 0.15, so each one cuts 0.15 into the podium behind it.
#
# THEY STAYED WHERE THEY WERE WHEN THE FLOOR WENT DOWN. A niche sill is 1.325 m over the new floor
# rather than 0.9125, which is where a lamp recess belongs and not where a seat does; and the eight
# flames are now 1.58 m up, which is eye height, and rake the coursed wall under them at a shallower
# angle than before. That is the case in load note 4 made harder rather than easier.
let crypt_niche_body = box  2.40 0.45 5.70    3.00 1.05 6.00
let crypt_niche_head = cylinder 2.70 1.05 5.85  r=0.30 h=0.30 axis=z
let crypt_niche_one  = union { crypt_niche_body crypt_niche_head }
let crypt_niche_pair = union { crypt_niche_one  translate { crypt_niche_one } 2.70 0 0 }
let crypt_niches = mirror { mirror { crypt_niche_pair } axis=x } axis=z

# --- the vault webs, cut UPWARDS ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 2.25 square (5 M) on the bay's 2.70 pitch, so the rib between two is 0.45 (D/2) — and every rib is
# centred on a column axis, which is where a vault's ribs belong.
#
# EACH WEB IS THE UNION OF TWO BARRELS AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT A GROIN. Radius 16.894 is the
# circle through the web's own springing at 1.6875, 1.125 from the bay's axis, and its crown at
# 1.725 — one line of arithmetic, and it is what turns a flat panel into a doubly curved web whose
# two halves meet on the bay's diagonals. The two cylinders are struck on the same centre and cross
# at right angles; the union of their insides is the groined web and the seam is the groin.
#
# Seven across and three deep — x 0, +-2.70, +-5.40, +-8.10 and z 0, +-2.70 — twenty-one of them,
# over the middle of the room, leaving a plain margin against all four walls where the aisles are.
let crypt_web_x = cylinder 0 -15.169 0  r=16.894 h=2.30 axis=x
let crypt_web_z = cylinder 0 -15.169 0  r=16.894 h=2.30 axis=z
let crypt_web_unit = intersection {
    union { crypt_web_x crypt_web_z }
    box -1.125 1.6800 -1.125   1.125 1.7250 1.125
}
let crypt_webs = repeat { repeat { crypt_web_unit } x=2.70 nx=3 } z=2.70 nz=1

let crypt_air = union {
    crypt_room crypt_slot_hollows crypt_wells crypt_niches crypt_webs
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE SHELL — one block of stone with the room taken out of it
# ================================================================================================
#
# ONE BLOCK WITH THE AIR TAKEN OUT OF IT, and not a floor, four walls and a ceiling unioned. A
# vaulted or a lined room is one piece of stone with a hole in it — halls.clip's own construction —
# and the reason to write it that way here is the floor: it is 0.175 m thick, and the part of it
# that is inside the sampled box is one voxel row at metre 32. A slab that thin written as its own
# box is a sheet of loose voxels at any coarse metre. Written as the bottom of a difference it is
# part of the same solid as the walls standing on it.
#
# THE BLOCK RUNS TO -1.05 AND THE BOX ONLY REACHES -0.90625 AT METRE 32. That is deliberate. The
# sampler rounds the contract's `bounds` outward to the enclosing voxel and gets a different floor
# at every metre — -1.000, -0.9375, -0.90625 — so a block written to any one of them has no stone
# under it at the other two. Written past all three it has exactly one solid row under the floor at
# each, and the run of air over the floor is closed at both ends at every metre.
#
# It is also why the water channel is KERBED AND NOT SUNK — a rill 0.15 deep would go through the
# floor of the building — and the kerbs are the better answer anyway, because they put a 0.1125 m
# granite arris beside the one specular surface in the room.
#
# The walls are 0.15 (M/3) and the ceiling 0.1125 (M/4), so the block is x +-11.85, z +-5.85 and
# y -1.05..1.80.
# `round=0.02` on it is the brief's small round on a stone arris.
let crypt_block = box -11.85 -1.05 -5.85   11.85 1.80 5.85 round=0.02

# --- the coursing -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE LINING IS LAID IN COURSES AND THE JOINTS ARE CUT, NOT PAINTED. A course is M tall, a joint is
# 0.030 wide and 0.030 deep — one voxel at the contract's metre — and the perpends are on 0.90 (D)
# with every second course offset by half of that, which is what stops an ashlar wall reading as a
# grid. podium.clip rusticates its own die exactly this way and for the same reason: a joint that is
# a CUT throws a shadow that moves with the light, and one that is a painted line does not.
#
# The bands are what stagger the perpends: two sets of 0.45 m slices 0.90 apart, one carrying the
# joints on the module and one carrying them on the half module, so no two courses in a row break
# their stones in the same place.
let crypt_joint_h  = box -12.00 -0.015 -9.30   12.00 0.015 6.10
let crypt_courses  = translate { repeat { crypt_joint_h } y=0.45 ny=6 } 0 -0.65 0

let crypt_band_seed = box -12.00 -0.20 -9.30   12.00 0.25 6.10
let crypt_band_even = repeat { crypt_band_seed } y=0.90 ny=3
let crypt_band_odd  = translate { repeat { crypt_band_seed } y=0.90 ny=3 } 0 0.45 0

let crypt_perp_x = box -0.015 -1.10 -9.30   0.015 1.80 6.10
let crypt_perp_z = box -12.00 -1.10 -0.015   12.00 1.80 0.015
let crypt_perps_x = union {
    intersection { repeat { crypt_perp_x } x=0.90 nx=14  crypt_band_even }
    intersection { translate { repeat { crypt_perp_x } x=0.90 nx=14 } 0.45 0 0  crypt_band_odd }
}
let crypt_perps_z = union {
    intersection { repeat { crypt_perp_z } z=0.90 nz=8  crypt_band_even }
    intersection { translate { repeat { crypt_perp_z } z=0.90 nz=8 } 0 0 0.45  crypt_band_odd }
}

# The four wall faces, 0.06 deep, so a joint is cut into the face and nowhere else — a full plane
# would slice the floor and the vault as well.
let crypt_face_n = box -11.70 -0.845 5.64    11.70 1.6875 5.70
let crypt_face_s = box -11.70 -0.845 -5.70   11.70 1.6875 -5.64
let crypt_face_e = box 11.64 -0.845 -5.70    11.70 1.6875 5.70
let crypt_face_w = box -11.70 -0.845 -5.70  -11.64 1.6875 5.70

let crypt_joints = union {
    intersection { union { crypt_courses crypt_perps_x } union { crypt_face_n crypt_face_s } }
    intersection { union { crypt_courses crypt_perps_z } union { crypt_face_e crypt_face_w } }
}

# --- the worn floor -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 0.030 m — one voxel at the contract's metre — taken out of the flags where feet actually fall: the
# long aisle on z = 0 that both stairs feed into, and a patch at the foot of each stair. It is a CUT
# and a coat, not a coat alone, because a hollow worn into a floor catches a raking flame on its far
# lip and a painted one does not.
#
# AND IT IS WHY THE FLOOR IS AT -0.845 AND THE SLAB'S TOP AT -0.875. Cut into a floor whose top was
# the slab's own top, the track took away the ONLY row of stone there is: the sampled box's own
# bottom moves with the metre, so at metre 32 there is exactly one voxel of floor under this room
# and a 30 mm hollow in it is a hole through the building. Laid ON the slab instead, the flags are
# a second row that a track can be worn through and the slab underneath is untouched — which is
# also what wear actually is. The track is the bed showing through, and it is real matter at metre
# 16 and at metre 32 and nothing at all at metre 8, where 30 mm is a quarter of a voxel.
let crypt_wear_aisle = box -11.10 -0.876 -0.45   11.10 -0.80 0.45
let crypt_wear_foot  = mirror { box 6.75 -0.876 4.35   8.10 -0.80 5.70 } axis=x
let crypt_wear = union { crypt_wear_aisle crypt_wear_foot }

let crypt_shell = difference {
    crypt_block
    crypt_room crypt_wells crypt_webs crypt_slot_hollows crypt_niches
    crypt_joints crypt_wear
}

# --- the corridors, lined the same way ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One block per corridor with its own air taken out of it: that gives floor, both walls, the end
# wall and the ceiling in one shape, with the shaft opening already cut in the ceiling. It runs
# 0.15 past the room's lining at -5.85 to -5.70 so the two are one solid.
# THE END WALL AND THE SIDE WALLS ARE 0.15 (M/3). When the floor was dropped for the head height the
# corridor's floor slab thinned, and a 0.05 end wall standing on a thin floor is two sub-voxel
# members meeting: the bottom course of all three corridor ends came away as 39 loose voxels the
# last time this was got wrong. Taking the block out to -9.15 gives the end wall the same M/3 the
# room's walls have. It cuts nothing extra — the air still stops at -9.00.
let crypt_slot_block_0 = box -1.05 -1.05 -9.15   1.05 1.80 -5.70
let crypt_slot_shell_0 = difference { crypt_slot_block_0 crypt_slot_hollow_0 }
let crypt_slot_shells = mirror {
    union { crypt_slot_shell_0  translate { crypt_slot_shell_0 } 4.05 0 0 }
} axis=x

# --- the archivolts on the three corridor mouths --------------------------------------------------
#
# A DOOR ARCHITRAVE WITH A REAL SECTION, and it is the one place in this room where a moulding is
# read against daylight. The band follows the segmental head and returns down both jambs to the
# floor: a 0.135 m archivolt standing 0.15 proud of the wall face at -5.70, with an impost block at
# the springing and a keystone at the crown. It is drawn as the difference of two struck circles —
# the outer 0.135 bigger than the inner, on the same centre — so the band is of constant width all
# the way round the head, which a stack of boxes cannot be.
#
# It stands on the wall, not in the opening, so it takes nothing off the 1.80 m clear width.
let crypt_arch_out = intersection {
    cylinder 0 -1.9125 -5.65  r=3.735 h=0.20 axis=z
    box -1.035 -0.92 -5.75    1.035 1.8225 -5.55
}
let crypt_arch_in = intersection {
    cylinder 0 -1.9125 -5.625  r=3.600 h=0.40 axis=z
    box -0.90 -0.95 -5.85      0.90 1.6875 -5.45
}
let crypt_arch_band = difference { crypt_arch_out crypt_arch_in }
let crypt_arch_imp  = mirror {
    box 0.90 1.5075 -5.75   1.10 1.6425 -5.49 round=0.010
} axis=x
let crypt_arch_key  = box -0.135 1.6425 -5.75   0.135 1.8225 -5.52 round=0.010
let crypt_arch_one  = union { crypt_arch_band crypt_arch_imp crypt_arch_key }
let crypt_archivolts = mirror {
    union { crypt_arch_one  translate { crypt_arch_one } 4.05 0 0 }
} axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE COLUMN — 2.5625 m from the floor to the springing, and every one of the forty is this shape
# ================================================================================================
#
#   -1.05   .. -0.5625  plinth, 0.675 square (1.5 M), everything under -0.875 in the floor slab
#   -0.5625 .. -0.45    splay, a cone standing on the block: the broach off square onto round
#   -0.45   ..  1.35    shaft, 0.45 diameter (M), no taper        4 D = 4 M
#    1.35   ..  1.545   cushion, from round below to square above
#    1.545  ..  1.6875  abacus, 0.63 square (1.4 M), running on to 1.7375 inside the ceiling
#
# 1.80 of shaft on 0.45 of diameter is FOUR DIAMETERS. The Ionic order upstairs is
# nine. That ratio is the whole of why this room reads as older than the building
# over it: it is not the same order made small, it is a different and cruder one, which is what a
# crypt under a neoclassical building always is. There is no entasis and no fluting, and the only
# moulding on the whole column is the splay at its foot and the cushion at its head — both of them
# the same trick, a cone and a block, one way up and then the other.
#
# THE CUSHION IS A CONE INTERSECTED WITH A CUBE, which is exactly what a cushion capital is: a
# block whose four lower faces are cut back to a circle so that a square abacus can sit on a round
# shaft. `cone` has its base at cy and its apex above it, so it is written upside down under the
# floor and turned half a turn about x, which maps y to -y exactly and keeps a bounding box. At
# 1.35 the cone's radius is 0.2565 against the shaft's 0.225, so the cushion oversails the shaft by
# 0.0315 and that lip is the capital's necking; at 1.575 it is 0.4275 against the block's
# half-diagonal of 0.4137, so all four corners of the block survive and the lunettes are true.
#
# THE SPLAY IS THE SAME FIGURE THE RIGHT WAY UP. A cone whose base radius is the plinth's own half
# width, standing on the plinth top at -0.5625, is inscribed in the square — so the four corners of
# the block stand proud of it and the stops between them are true broaches. Its radius is exactly
# the shaft's at -0.45, which is the podium's own underside, and above that it is thinner than the
# shaft and buried in it, which is why it needs no clipping.
let crypt_col_plinth = box -0.3375 -1.05 -0.3375   0.3375 -0.5625 0.3375 round=0.012
let crypt_col_splay  = cone 0 -0.60 0  r=0.3375 h=0.45 axis=y
let crypt_col_shaft  = cylinder 0 0.50 0  r=0.225 h=1.90 axis=y
let crypt_col_cone   = rotate { cone 0 -1.575 0  r=0.4275 h=0.5625 axis=y } x=0.5
let crypt_col_block  = box -0.2925 1.35 -0.2925   0.2925 1.575 0.2925 round=0.010
let crypt_col_cush   = intersection { crypt_col_block crypt_col_cone }
let crypt_col_abacus = box -0.315 1.545 -0.315   0.315 1.7375 0.315 round=0.010

let crypt_col_unit = union {
    crypt_col_shaft crypt_col_plinth crypt_col_splay crypt_col_cush crypt_col_abacus
}

# The grid. A repeat of a child 0.675 wide inside a 2.70 period is exact and keeps its bound; the
# seed sits at x = 0 so nothing straddles a cell boundary, and the finished line is carried out by
# half a bay afterwards so the axes land on 1.35, 4.05, 6.75 and 9.45. Then folded twice.
let crypt_col_row  = mirror { translate { repeat { crypt_col_unit } x=2.70 nx=3 } 1.35 0 0 } axis=x
let crypt_columns  = mirror {
    union { translate { crypt_col_row } 0 0 1.35   translate { crypt_col_row } 0 0 4.05 }
} axis=z

# THE RESPONDS — the same column cut in half down its axis and set against the east and west walls
# on the four column rows, so every row is stopped by a shaft instead of dying against flat stone,
# and so that the four transverse ribs have a capital to land on.
# The half is taken on -x and carried to +11.70, which puts its flat face on the wall and its round
# face in the room; `mirror axis=x` then puts the matching eight on the west wall.
let crypt_resp_half = intersection { crypt_col_unit  box -0.60 -1.10 -0.60   0 1.80 0.60 }
let crypt_resp_col  = translate { crypt_resp_half } 11.70 0 0
let crypt_responds  = mirror {
    mirror { union { translate { crypt_resp_col } 0 0 1.35   translate { crypt_resp_col } 0 0 4.05 } } axis=z
} axis=x

let crypt_shafts_all = union { crypt_columns crypt_responds }

# ================================================================================================
# THE RIBS — a moulded section, on the column axes, springing off the capitals
# ================================================================================================
#
# Three members and a real section, because a rib drawn as one box is a beam:
#
#   1.6375 .. 1.7375   the band, M wide, and 0.05 of it INSIDE the ceiling slab so that the rib is
#                      one solid with what it hangs from rather than touching it on a plane
#   1.587  .. 1.6875   the keel, 0.27 wide (0.6 M), the member the eye actually reads, and it runs
#                      the whole way up into the band rather than meeting it on a plane
#   a roll of 0.045 radius down each re-entrant, at +-0.135, which is the arris a raking taper
#   finds and the reason the rib does not read as a rectangle in section
#
# NOTHING HANGS BELOW 1.587. `round=` does not grow a box, so the keel's soffit is the 1.587 written
# in it; the rolls are struck at 1.6375 with a radius of 0.045, so their lowest point is 1.5925. See
# the head-height note at the top: 1.587 is worth 2.4375 m under a rib at metre 16 and 2.500 at
# metre 8, and 1.5625 would be worth 2.375 at metre 8 and would fail.
#
# Eight ribs run in z on x = +-1.35, +-4.05, +-6.75 and +-9.45, wall to wall; four run in x on
# z = +-1.35 and +-4.05, respond to respond. They cross over every capital in the room.
let crypt_rib_band_z = box -0.225 1.6375 -5.70   0.225 1.7375 5.70
let crypt_rib_keel_z = box -0.135 1.5870 -5.70   0.135 1.6875 5.70 round=0.012
let crypt_rib_rl_z_a = cylinder  0.135 1.6375 0  r=0.045 h=11.40 axis=z
let crypt_rib_rl_z_b = cylinder -0.135 1.6375 0  r=0.045 h=11.40 axis=z
let crypt_rib_z_one  = union { crypt_rib_band_z crypt_rib_keel_z crypt_rib_rl_z_a crypt_rib_rl_z_b }
let crypt_ribs_z = mirror {
    translate { repeat { crypt_rib_z_one } x=2.70 nx=3 } 1.35 0 0
} axis=x

let crypt_rib_band_x = box -11.70 1.6375 -0.225   11.70 1.7375 0.225
let crypt_rib_keel_x = box -11.70 1.5870 -0.135   11.70 1.6875 0.135 round=0.012
let crypt_rib_rl_x_a = cylinder 0 1.6375  0.135  r=0.045 h=23.40 axis=x
let crypt_rib_rl_x_b = cylinder 0 1.6375 -0.135  r=0.045 h=23.40 axis=x
let crypt_rib_x_one  = union { crypt_rib_band_x crypt_rib_keel_x crypt_rib_rl_x_a crypt_rib_rl_x_b }
let crypt_ribs_x = mirror {
    translate { repeat { crypt_rib_x_one } z=2.70 nz=1 } 0 0 1.35
} axis=z

let crypt_ribs = difference { union { crypt_ribs_z crypt_ribs_x } crypt_wells }

# ================================================================================================
# THE STAIRS — fourteen risers of 0.18 and a last step of 0.125, in the north aisle at each end
# ================================================================================================
#
# Fourteen treads of the building's 0.32 run from x = 7.22 up to 11.70, each 1.125 wide (2.5 M) in
# the north aisle between the column row at z = 4.05 and the wall at 5.70 — 0.1875 clear of the
# nearest plinth. Nested boxes, each running from its own riser EAST to the common edge at 11.70 so
# that every box below is buried in the ones above, and every one of them reaching down to the floor
# slab: this is a solid spandrel, not a raking slab, so there is no space under it for anybody to
# stand up in and nothing for the head-height question to be asked about.
#
# IT IS BEDDED 0.05 INTO THE EAST WALL AND 0.05 INTO THE NORTH WALL, which is the brief's overlap
# and not decoration. Written to stop exactly on the wall faces at 11.70 and 5.70 it reported TWO
# FLOATING VOXELS at metre 8 — one at each stair's outer bottom corner, where a thin floor slab and
# a stair that only touches the wall quantize apart. 11.75 and 5.75 put them back.
#
# EVERY NUMBER IS THE FACE IT MAKES, and it did not used to be. `round=` does NOT grow a box — see
# the note at the top — so the old advice to write every number 0.012 inside its own face made this
# stair 12 mm shallow at every tread and 24 mm short at both walls. The tread tops here ARE the
# tread tops, and the top one at 1.675 leaves the 0.125 the arithmetic demands.
let crypt_st_01 = box   7.22 -0.925 4.575   11.75  -0.665 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_02 = box   7.54 -0.925 4.575   11.75  -0.485 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_03 = box   7.86 -0.925 4.575   11.75  -0.305 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_04 = box   8.18 -0.925 4.575   11.75  -0.125 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_05 = box   8.50 -0.925 4.575   11.75   0.055 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_06 = box   8.82 -0.925 4.575   11.75   0.235 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_07 = box   9.14 -0.925 4.575   11.75   0.415 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_08 = box   9.46 -0.925 4.575   11.75   0.595 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_09 = box   9.78 -0.925 4.575   11.75   0.775 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_10 = box  10.10 -0.925 4.575   11.75   0.955 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_11 = box  10.42 -0.925 4.575   11.75   1.135 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_12 = box  10.74 -0.925 4.575   11.75   1.315 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_13 = box  11.06 -0.925 4.575   11.75   1.495 5.75 round=0.012
let crypt_st_14 = box  11.38 -0.925 4.575   11.75   1.675 5.75 round=0.012

let crypt_stair_e = union {
    crypt_st_01 crypt_st_02 crypt_st_03 crypt_st_04 crypt_st_05 crypt_st_06 crypt_st_07
    crypt_st_08 crypt_st_09 crypt_st_10 crypt_st_11 crypt_st_12 crypt_st_13 crypt_st_14
}
let crypt_stairs = mirror { crypt_stair_e } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE GRATINGS — the point of the whole room, and one voxel thick
# ================================================================================================
#
# Seven bars of 0.030 x 0.090 at 0.150 centres (M/3) across an opening 1.20 x 1.00, in a rim 0.05
# wide that is bedded in the shaft's own ceiling. The bars run east-west so the stripes they throw
# run across the corridor and up the wall at its end, where there is something for them to land on.
#
# The rim is 0.05 into the stone on all four sides, which is the brief's overlap, and the bars run
# 0.03 into the rim at each end. Nothing here floats and nothing here is thinner than a bar has to
# be: iron at rough=150 metal=180, so each bar is also a specular highlight one voxel wide seen
# against the brightest thing in the room.
# THE RIM STARTS AT 1.71 AND NOT FLUSH WITH THE PODIUM'S OWN PAVING, and the 0.0225 between them is
# a ring of stone that comes away: `crypt-place` — the podium with this room cut out of it — reports
# 36 voxels adrift at metre 16, and they are that ring, sub-voxel in two directions at once.
# Bringing the rim down flush was the obvious fix and MADE IT WORSE, because then the rim's own
# underside lands on a face of the shaft and sheds slivers of its own: 72 adrift in `crypt-place`
# and 144 in the part, against 36 and 0. So it stays at 1.71. The 36 are pre-existing and they are
# buried inside podium stone at the mouth of a shaft where nothing can see them. Measured both ways
# rather than reasoned, because reasoning got it backwards.
let crypt_grate_rim_0 = difference {
    box -0.65 1.71 -9.05    0.65 1.80 -7.95
    box -0.60 1.68 -9.00    0.60 1.83 -8.00
}
let crypt_grate_bar_0 = box -0.63 1.71 -0.015   0.63 1.80 0.015
let crypt_grate_bars_0 = translate { repeat { crypt_grate_bar_0 } z=0.15 nz=3 } 0 0 -8.50
let crypt_grate_0 = union { crypt_grate_rim_0 crypt_grate_bars_0 }
let crypt_gratings = mirror {
    union { crypt_grate_0  translate { crypt_grate_0 } 4.05 0 0 }
} axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE WATER — a basin against the north wall, a channel down the axis, a sump under the middle light
# ================================================================================================
#
# The channel runs 13.58 m along x = 0, which is a clear aisle because the column axes are odd
# multiples of the bay's half. It starts at a basin bedded into the north lining, fed by a bronze
# spout through a thread of water 0.135 m across — four voxels, refracting, falling 1.25 m — and it
# ends in a sump directly under the middle grating, which is the one place in this room where
# daylight and water are in the same square metre.
#
# THE SPOUT DID NOT GO DOWN WITH THE FLOOR. It is where it was, at y = 0.60, which is now 1.475 m
# over the floor instead of 1.06, so the thread is half as long again and falls past a coursed wall
# for the whole of it. A falling thread of water in front of a joint that is one voxel deep is the
# refraction case and the thin-matter case in the same square metre.
#
# KERBED, NOT SUNK. See the floor note above: the floor slab is thin and a sunk channel would go
# through it. The kerbs are 0.1125 (M/4) tall and 0.1125 wide and the water lies flush in the flags
# between them, so the channel reads as a channel from standing height and the wet strip still
# catches a flame at ten degrees.
# THE GAP. The rills run INTO the basin and the sump rather than up against them, so a 0.225 notch
# the width of the channel is cut through each rim — from the sump's centre to the basin's centre,
# which is one box and cannot notch the far side of either by accident.
let crypt_ch_gap = box -0.1125 -1.05 -8.55   0.1125 -0.7125 5.025

let crypt_ch_kerb = mirror { box 0.1125 -0.9125 -8.25   0.225 -0.75 4.425 round=0.008 } axis=x
# THE RILL'S WATER IS FLUSH WITH THE FLAGS, AND IT IS THE ONE THING IN THIS ROOM THAT LOOKS LIKE A
# COMPROMISE AND IS NOT.
#
# The rill runs down the middle of the x = 0 aisle — the one clear axis in the room, the one you
# walk in along from the stair — so a foot lands on it whether or not anybody meant it to. Filled to
# the depth water looks it measured a clear run 0.0625 short over the whole centre line and
# clipcheck flagged every point of it, because A HORIZONTAL SLAB OF MATTER THINNER THAN A VOXEL IS
# DILATED BY THE SAMPLER a whole voxel in each direction: `--part crypt_ch_water` at metre 16 once
# reported a worldbox 0.188 m tall for a box 0.030 m tall.
#
# So the water is drawn -0.9125 .. -0.875: the top layer of the floor slab itself, between two
# granite kerbs 0.1125 high. It is wet stone rather than a filled trough, which is what ground
# water lying in a channel actually looks like. The BASIN and the SUMP keep their full 0.22 m of
# depth — they are vessels with a kerb round them, nobody stands in one, and they are where the
# water is meant to be seen as water.
let crypt_ch_water = box -0.1125 -0.9125 -8.55   0.1125 -0.845 5.025

let crypt_basin_kerb = difference {
    cylinder 0 -0.775 5.025  r=0.75  h=0.275 axis=y
    cylinder 0 -0.775 5.025  r=0.525 h=0.350 axis=y
    crypt_ch_gap
}
let crypt_basin_water = cylinder 0 -0.7725 5.025  r=0.525 h=0.24 axis=y

let crypt_sump_kerb = difference {
    cylinder 0 -0.775 -8.55  r=0.45  h=0.275 axis=y
    cylinder 0 -0.775 -8.55  r=0.225 h=0.350 axis=y
    crypt_ch_gap
}
let crypt_sump_water = cylinder 0 -0.7725 -8.55  r=0.225 h=0.24 axis=y

# THE SPOUT IS 0.18 ACROSS (0.4 M) AND THE FALL 0.135 (0.3 M), AND BOTH ARE BIGGER THAN THEY WANT
# TO BE. A jet 0.036 across is what the eye expects and it is under a voxel at metre 8: it renders
# as a broken string of loose voxels that the component count correctly calls floating, and at
# every metre below 32 it is not there at all. The thin matter this room is here to test is the
# grating bar, which is a fixed 0.030 because it is fixed to something at both ends; a free-falling
# rod has to survive on its own. 0.135 is four voxels at metre 32 — still a thread beside a 0.45 m
# column — and it is the smallest free-standing round solid in the building that is certainly there.
let crypt_spout  = cylinder 0 0.60  5.55  r=0.09   h=0.60 axis=z
let crypt_thread = cylinder 0 -0.05 5.30  r=0.0675 h=1.40 axis=y

let crypt_stonework_wet = union { crypt_ch_kerb crypt_basin_kerb crypt_sump_kerb }
let crypt_water = union { crypt_ch_water crypt_basin_water crypt_sump_water crypt_thread }

# ================================================================================================
# THE TOMB CHESTS — four, in the outer bays of the east and west aisles
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.90 (D) by 2.025 (4.5 M) on a plinth, with a half-round lid struck on the chest's own half-width
# so the section is a true semicircle and not a guessed curve. They stand on x = +-10.575 in the
# middle of the 2.025 m aisle, on z = +-2.70 which is a bay centre, so each one is framed by four
# columns and stands under the crossing of two ribs.
#
#   -0.9125 .. -0.6875   plinth, M/2, the bottom M/12 in the floor slab
#   -0.6875 ..  0.0625   chest, 0.75 tall, and every face of it worked
#    0.0625 ..  0.5125   lid, a half round of M struck on the chest
#
# WHAT IS ON THE CHEST, AND WHY IT IS CUT AND NOT PAINTED. Five blind arcades sunk 0.030 into each
# long face — a shaft 0.27 wide with a semicircular head of 0.135, on a 0.405 pitch, which is what
# the side of a Romanesque tomb chest is — and above them an inscription of nine letters, HIC IACET,
# whose strokes are 0.036 across and 0.15 tall, incised 0.030. Both of those are ONE VOXEL DEEP at
# the contract's metre and vanish entirely at metre 8. They are matter because a 30 mm sinking in
# stone IS matter: it has a lip that a taper catches and a floor that stays dark, and no coat of
# paint has either. The alternative — a painted letter — is a decal, and this building has a rule
# about that.
#
# TWO IN PORPHYRY AND TWO IN ROSSO, and which is which is decided by z and not by x, because the
# building is symmetric about x = 0 and a red tomb facing a purple one across the room would break
# that. North pair porphyry (118,52,54), south pair rosso (124,44,42) — six values apart in red and
# eight in green, which is a difference you can only make in one frame against the same light, and
# that is the point of putting them in the same room.
let crypt_sarc_plinth = box 10.0125 -0.9500  1.575   11.1375 -0.6875 3.825  round=0.012
let crypt_sarc_chest  = box 10.1250 -0.7500  1.6875  11.0250  0.0625 3.7125 round=0.015
let crypt_sarc_lid = intersection {
    cylinder 10.575 0.0625 2.70  r=0.45 h=2.025 axis=z
    box 10.125 0.0000 1.6875   11.025 0.5125 3.7125
}
let crypt_sarc_mass = union { crypt_sarc_plinth crypt_sarc_chest crypt_sarc_lid }

# --- the blind arcading, one bay drawn at z = 0 and repeated ---------------------------------------
let crypt_sarc_arc_w = union {
    box 10.110 -0.6275 -0.135   10.155 -0.3000 0.135
    cylinder 10.1325 -0.3000 0  r=0.135 h=0.135 axis=x
}
let crypt_sarc_arc_e = union {
    box 10.995 -0.6275 -0.135   11.040 -0.3000 0.135
    cylinder 11.0175 -0.3000 0  r=0.135 h=0.135 axis=x
}
let crypt_sarc_arcade = translate {
    repeat { union { crypt_sarc_arc_w crypt_sarc_arc_e } } z=0.405 nz=2
} 0 0 2.70

# --- the inscription, HIC IACET, cut into the west face ---------------------------------------------
#
# Nine cells of 0.20 on the letter line, from z = 1.90 to z = 3.50, with the fourth left blank for
# the word space. Every letter is built out of the same two strokes — a vertical 0.036 wide and a
# horizontal 0.036 tall — except C, which is a ring with its right-hand quarter taken out, and A,
# which is two capsules and a bar. That is the whole of a Roman monumental alphabet's construction
# and it is why an inscription is worth cutting rather than painting: the letter is a V-shaped
# trench, and what makes it legible on stone is the shadow in it.
#
# Everything is written from x = 10.110 to 10.155, so the cut is 0.030 deep into a face at 10.125
# with 0.015 of slack outside it, and it cannot reach the far side of a chest 0.90 m thick.
let crypt_let_v  = box 10.110 -0.10 -0.018   10.155 0.05 0.018
let crypt_let_h  = box 10.110 -0.018 -0.075   10.155 0.018 0.075

let crypt_let_I = crypt_let_v
let crypt_let_H = union {
    translate { crypt_let_v } 0 0 -0.057
    translate { crypt_let_v } 0 0  0.057
    translate { crypt_let_h } 0 -0.025 0
}
let crypt_let_T = union {
    crypt_let_v
    translate { crypt_let_h } 0 0.032 0
}
let crypt_let_E = union {
    translate { crypt_let_v } 0 0 -0.057
    translate { crypt_let_h } 0  0.032 0.018
    translate { crypt_let_h } 0 -0.025 0.018
    translate { crypt_let_h } 0 -0.082 0.018
}
let crypt_let_C = difference {
    cylinder 10.1325 -0.025 0  r=0.075 h=0.135 axis=x
    cylinder 10.1325 -0.025 0  r=0.039 h=0.180 axis=x
    box 10.080 -0.045 0.030   10.185 -0.005 0.120
}
let crypt_let_A = union {
    capsule 10.1325 -0.10 -0.060   10.1325 0.05 0.000 r=0.018
    capsule 10.1325 -0.10  0.060   10.1325 0.05 0.000 r=0.018
    translate { crypt_let_h } 0 -0.045 0
}

let crypt_inscription = union {
    translate { crypt_let_H } 0 0 1.90
    translate { crypt_let_I } 0 0 2.10
    translate { crypt_let_C } 0 0 2.30
    translate { crypt_let_I } 0 0 2.70
    translate { crypt_let_A } 0 0 2.90
    translate { crypt_let_C } 0 0 3.10
    translate { crypt_let_E } 0 0 3.30
    translate { crypt_let_T } 0 0 3.50
}

let crypt_sarc_one = difference {
    crypt_sarc_mass  crypt_sarc_arcade crypt_inscription
}
let crypt_sarc_n = mirror { crypt_sarc_one } axis=x
let crypt_sarc_s = mirror { translate { crypt_sarc_one } 0 0 -5.40 } axis=x
let crypt_sarcophagi = union { crypt_sarc_n crypt_sarc_s }

# ================================================================================================
# THE TAPERS — eight, one to a niche, and the only light in the room that does not come down a shaft
# ================================================================================================
#
# The taper stands at z = 5.775, which is INSIDE the lining (5.70..5.85) and not in the niche's own
# depth, so its foot is bedded 0.03 into stone of mine rather than standing on the podium behind me
# — the difference between a candle and a candle that this part cannot prove it owns.
let crypt_taper_wax   = cylinder 2.70 0.555 5.775  r=0.030 h=0.270 axis=y
let crypt_taper_flame = ellipsoid 2.70 0.705 5.775  rx=0.024 ry=0.048 rz=0.024
let crypt_taper_one   = union { crypt_taper_wax crypt_taper_flame }
let crypt_taper_pair  = union { crypt_taper_one  translate { crypt_taper_one } 2.70 0 0 }
let crypt_tapers_all  = mirror { mirror { crypt_taper_pair } axis=x } axis=z

let crypt_wax_all = mirror { mirror {
    union { crypt_taper_wax  translate { crypt_taper_wax } 2.70 0 0 }
} axis=x } axis=z
let crypt_flame_all = mirror { mirror {
    union { crypt_taper_flame  translate { crypt_taper_flame } 2.70 0 0 }
} axis=x } axis=z

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART, AND THE VOID
# ================================================================================================

let part_crypt = union {
    crypt_shell crypt_slot_shells crypt_archivolts
    crypt_shafts_all crypt_ribs crypt_stairs
    crypt_gratings crypt_sarcophagi
    crypt_stonework_wet crypt_water crypt_spout
    crypt_tapers_all
}

# THE AIR OF THIS ROOM LESS MY OWN STONE, AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHAT IT MUST BE. Written as a
# difference against part_crypt rather than as a list of boxes, for the reason halls.clip and
# vestibule.clip both give: it takes away anything another fragment leaves standing in my room, it
# cannot touch a stone of mine, and it cannot go stale when a member is added to the part above.
let void_crypt = difference { crypt_air part_crypt }

# ================================================================================================
# THE WALKING BANDS, AS SHAPES, BECAUSE A HEIGHT TAKEN AT ONE POINT IS NOT A ROOM
# ================================================================================================
#
# `--gap y@x,z` answers one column. A room is not a column, and the place a vaulted room fails is
# never the one you would have chosen to probe. So the circulation rule — 1.20 m of width and 2.40 m
# of head — is also built as the VOLUME A PERSON SWEEPS, intersected with this part, WHICH MUST
# MEASURE EMPTY. That is BRIEF.md rule 7's own advice for anything a heightfield cannot walk, and it
# is the only thing that checks the WHOLE length of an aisle rather than a point on it.
#
#   ./build/clipcheck clips/facility/requests/crypt-probe.clip --metre 32 --part crypt_walk_x
#   ./build/clipcheck clips/facility/requests/crypt-probe.clip --metre 32 --part crypt_walk_z
#   ./build/clipcheck clips/facility/requests/crypt-probe.clip --metre 32 --part crypt_walk_slot
#
# All three are 1.20 m wide and run to 1.555, which is 2.40 m over the flags at -0.845. 1.555 is
# 0.032 under the rib soffit at 1.587 and 0.010 under the abacus foot at 1.545 — so the lanes are
# laid between the column axes rather than across them, which is what the aisles are.
#
# THEY START 0.30 ABOVE THE FLOOR AND NOT ON IT, AND THAT IS THE WATER AND NOTHING ELSE. Run from
# -0.845 the three lanes came back with 1368 voxels in them and every one was a granite kerb: the
# rill's two kerbs stand 0.1125 above the flags and the sump's rim 0.1625, and the rill crosses both
# long aisles and runs the length of the middle light-well. A kerb is a STEP — the rule is 0.55 and
# these are a fifth of it — and it is not a head-height obstruction, so the band that answers the
# head-height question starts over the top of them at -0.545. Below that line the only matter
# standing in any lane in this room is those kerbs. Measured, not asserted: the 1368 voxels were the
# reason the number moved.
#
#   crypt_walk_x     the two long aisles on z = +-3.00, wall to wall in x short of the tomb chests
#   crypt_walk_z     the two cross aisles on x = +-3.00, wall to wall in z
#   crypt_walk_slot  the three light-well corridors, the full 3.30 m of each
let crypt_walk_x = mirror { box -9.60 -0.545 2.40    9.60 1.555 3.60 } axis=z
let crypt_walk_z = mirror { box  2.40 -0.545 -5.10   3.60 1.555 5.10 } axis=x
let crypt_walk_slot = mirror {
    union { box -0.60 -0.545 -8.90   0.60 1.555 -5.70
            box  3.45 -0.545 -8.90   4.65 1.555 -5.70 }
} axis=x

let crypt_walk = intersection { part_crypt  union { crypt_walk_x crypt_walk_z crypt_walk_slot } }

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Later coats cover earlier ones, so: the shell first, then the floor and its bands and the tracks
# worn in it, then the vault and its ribs, then everything standing on the floor, then the metals
# and the flames. Every rule is keyed on one of my own shapes and every one is below=0.02 and never
# 0 — the manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm of grain at the end, and a test at zero
# leaves a rind of the base limestone coat over every outward face of this part. Anything a
# transform put where it is is at 0.035, which is BRIEF.md rule 5's second number.
#
# THE WALLS ARE TUFF AND NOTHING ELSE IN THE BUILDING USES IT. 150,142,124 at rough=250: the
# roughest, deadest surface in the contract, which is what an unrendered undercroft wall is and
# which is also the one thing that makes a taper 0.075 m off it worth putting there. Against it:
# granite columns and ribs at 225, travertine floor at 228, onyx where feet have polished it at 90,
# limestone vault at 205, and water at 24. That is a five-stop roughness ladder inside one room lit
# by one kind of light.

let crypt_zone_shell = union { crypt_shell crypt_slot_shells crypt_archivolts }
paint tuff where=crypt_zone_shell below=0.02

let crypt_zone_floor = intersection {
    union { crypt_shell crypt_slot_shells }
    box -12.00 -0.96 -9.20   12.00 -0.82 5.90
}
paint travertine where=crypt_zone_floor below=0.02

# The bands: 0.45 (M) wide, laid on the column axes in both directions, so the floor's pattern is
# the vault's pattern turned over and every band runs from shaft to shaft. Same repeat-translate-
# fold as the columns themselves, so the two can never drift apart.
let crypt_band_x = mirror { translate { repeat { box -0.225 -0.96 -6.00   0.225 -0.82 6.00 } x=2.70 nx=3 } 1.35 0 0 } axis=x
let crypt_band_z = mirror { translate { repeat { box -12.00 -0.96 -0.225  12.00 -0.82 0.225 } z=2.70 nz=1 } 0 0 1.35 } axis=z
let crypt_zone_band = intersection {
    crypt_shell
    union { crypt_band_x crypt_band_z }
    box -12.00 -0.96 -5.90   12.00 -0.82 5.90
}
paint granite where=crypt_zone_band below=0.02

# The tracks worn in the flags, painted over the bands they cross because a foot does not care
# which stone it is on. `onyx` is 206,186,150 against travertine's 214,196,164 — eight values apart
# and a hundred and thirty-eight rougher, which is the same stone polished and nothing else.
let crypt_zone_wear = intersection {
    crypt_shell
    union { box -11.10 -0.92 -0.45   11.10 -0.855 0.45
            mirror { box 6.75 -0.92 4.35   8.10 -0.855 5.70 } axis=x }
}
paint onyx where=crypt_zone_wear below=0.035

let crypt_zone_ceiling = intersection {
    union { crypt_shell crypt_slot_shells }
    box -12.00 1.55 -9.20   12.00 1.85 5.90
}
paint limestone where=crypt_zone_ceiling below=0.02

paint granite where=crypt_ribs below=0.035
paint granite where=crypt_shafts_all below=0.02
paint granite where=crypt_stairs below=0.02
paint granite where=crypt_stonework_wet below=0.02

paint porphyry where=crypt_sarc_n below=0.02
paint rosso    where=crypt_sarc_s below=0.02

paint iron   where=crypt_gratings below=0.02
paint bronze where=crypt_spout below=0.02
paint water  where=crypt_water below=0.02

paint wax   where=crypt_wax_all below=0.02
paint taper where=crypt_flame_all below=0.02

# --- the damp, and the salt over it ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `on=` IS NOT OPTIONAL and it is the difference between moss on my lowest courses and a bleached
# lawn twenty metres away: without it the coats are keyed on a value rather than on a place and
# they go on over the whole clip after everything anybody painted. The zone is my own part
# intersected with the bottom 1.075 m of the room, which is where ground water actually shows —
# along the channel, round the basin, up the plinths, and not on the vault.
#
# It stops at 0.20, which is below the niche sills at 0.45 and a long way below the gratings at
# 1.71, so neither a taper nor a one-voxel bar is displaced by it.
let crypt_damp = intersection {
    part_crypt
    box -12.00 -0.96 -9.20   12.00 0.20 5.90
}
weather overgrown 0.16 scale=0.70 seed=61 on=crypt_damp

# --- the salt, and the wet stain under it ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE OTHER HALF OF WHAT A CELLAR WALL DOES, AND IT IS PAINT BECAUSE EFFLORESCENCE IS PAINT.
# Salt bloom is a bloom ON a surface and not a change to it: it has no thickness a sampler can hold
# at any metre this building is drawn at, and the rule about matter cuts both ways. A joint is 30 mm
# of stone that is not there and it is CUT; a salt crust is 0.2 mm of crystal and it is PAINTED.
#
# `weather sea` was tried first and is the wrong tool: its coats come as a package with barnacles
# and weed, and at the amount that makes any salt at all the crypt went 8% green. So the two stains
# are built here instead, as ZONES OF MY OWN, and that keeps rule 5 as well:
#
# A BLOTCH IS A SOLID WHOSE SURFACE WANDERS. `displace` a box that stands just clear of the wall
# face by an fbm bigger than the gap it left, and the box crosses the face in patches and stays
# clear of it between them; intersect that with my own shell and the result is a zone that is a
# real shape, keyed on nothing but geometry, and paintable with `where=` like any other. The two
# use the same pattern at different scales and seeds so the salt does not trace the damp.
#
#   crypt_dank   -0.96 .. -0.20  a dark wet skin at the foot of everything, in granite against the
#                                tuff: 24 values darker and 25 rougher the other way
#   crypt_bloom  -0.35 ..  0.95  the efflorescence over it, in plaster — 226,222,212 against
#                                150,142,124, which is the whitest thing in the room after a flame
#
# They overlap by 0.15 m on purpose: a damp line is not a line.
let crypt_stain_low  = fbm size=0.75 octaves=4 seed=73
let crypt_stain_high = fbm size=0.45 octaves=4 seed=79

let crypt_dank_core = box -11.63 -0.96 -5.63   11.63 -0.20 5.63
let crypt_dank_blob = displace { crypt_dank_core crypt_stain_low } amount=0.30
let crypt_dank = intersection {
    union { crypt_shell crypt_shafts_all crypt_stonework_wet }
    crypt_dank_blob
    box -11.86 -0.96 -5.86   11.86 -0.20 5.86
}
paint granite where=crypt_dank below=0.035

let crypt_bloom_core = box -11.63 -0.35 -5.63   11.63 0.95 5.63
let crypt_bloom_blob = displace { crypt_bloom_core crypt_stain_high } amount=0.30
let crypt_bloom = intersection {
    crypt_shell
    crypt_bloom_blob
    box -11.86 -0.35 -5.86   11.86 0.95 5.86
}
paint plaster where=crypt_bloom below=0.035
